flowchart TD
  subgraph L0["Level 0 · Question"]
    Q["<b>Why does the U.S. trade balance still matter,<br/>even if deficits can accompany strong growth?</b><br/><i>Brad Setser, CFR, Dec 2017</i>"]
  end

  subgraph L1["Level 1 · Priors & assumptions"]
    P1["<b>Net exports contribute to GDP growth</b><br/>Changes in exports and imports mechanically affect output growth"]
    P2["<b>Trade shapes employment composition</b><br/>Goods-producing sectors and regions are unevenly exposed"]
    P3["<b>Persistent deficits accumulate external debt</b><br/>Ongoing borrowing raises the net external debt stock"]
  end

  subgraph L2["Level 2 · Evidence"]
    D1["<b>2014–16 net-export drag</b><br/>Net exports subtracted about 1.5pp from U.S. growth"]
    D2["<b>Manufacturing employment varies with trade</b><br/>Surplus economies retain larger manufacturing job shares"]
    D3["<b>Manufacturing balance did not drive adjustment</b><br/>Lower oil imports and offshore income reduced the current-account deficit"]
    D4["<b>External debt rose from ~20% to ~50% of GDP</b><br/>Debt increased even as U.S. borrowing costs fell"]
    D5["<b>Higher rates or wider deficits raise debt ratios</b><br/>Either would weaken current favorable debt dynamics"]
    D6["<b>Tax cuts could widen trade deficit</b><br/>Setser estimates roughly 0.3–0.5% of GDP over several years"]
  end

  subgraph L3["Level 3 · Mechanisms"]
    M1["<b>Trade deficits can drag on growth</b><br/>More demand leaks abroad through net imports"]
    M2["<b>Trade shifts where jobs are located</b><br/>Manufacturing deficits reduce employment in exposed sectors and regions"]
    M3["<b>Deficits build external debt</b><br/>Persistent borrowing plus higher interest costs raise the external debt burden"]
  end

  subgraph L4["Level 4 · Intermediate claims"]
    C1["<b>Trade changes the pace of growth</b><br/>A stronger economy can coexist with a deficit, but net exports still affect realized GDP growth"]
    C2["<b>Trade changes the composition of employment</b><br/>Its effects are sectoral and regional even near full employment"]
    C3["<b>Current debt stability is conditional</b><br/>It depends on favorable growth, rates, and offshore income continuing"]
  end

  subgraph L5["Level 5 · Conclusion"]
    K["<b>Main claim: the trade balance remains worth watching</b><br/>It contains information about growth, employment composition,<br/>and long-run external debt sustainability"]
  end

  subgraph L6["Level 6 · Dialectic"]
    X["<b>Opposing view</b><br/>A wider deficit can reflect stronger domestic demand rather than weakness"]
    QF1["<b>Measurement caveat</b><br/>Monthly trade data are noisy and should be read as a time series"]
    QF2["<b>Employment caveat</b><br/>Near full employment, trade changes job distribution more than total employment"]
    R1["<b>Debt caveat</b><br/>The U.S. can sustain more external debt than most because of the dollar's global role"]
  end

  P1 --> M1
  D1 --> M1
  D6 --> M1
  M1 --> C1

  P2 --> M2
